      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14206876/assets/6e12ca457954f93778b772f2d0bd5a1e63e7e8e7eaf626d309300e97b23ab525.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the boxes in the statement below?\n\nThe ratio of hexagons to rhombuses is $\\boxed{}:1$ because there are $\\boxed{}$ hexagons for every $1$ rhombus.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we count the number of hexagons. We see that there are $4$ hexagons.\n\nThen, we count the number of rhombuses. We see that there are $2$ rhombuses.\n\nSo the ratio of hexagons to rhombuses is $4:2.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,4 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,2 &  \\\\ \\,\\,2 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,1 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $2:1$ is equivalent to $4:2.$ \n\nSo, the complete statement is\n\"The ratio of hexagons to rhombuses is $2:1$ because there are $2$ hexagons for every $1$ rhombus.\"",
